content.text:
{
  "id": "BCN-207",
  "title": "beacon-status flags a full battery and misses a nearly flat one",
  "body": "This morning Skellan Rock showed WARN for battery 100% while Dunvarrow Light at 9% showed OK. Looks like the battery check compares the numbers as text. Low-battery beacons are the ones we actually need to drive out to.",
  "labels": [
    "bug"
  ],
  "state": "open",
  "comments": [
    {
      "body": "Cause: bin/beacon-status checked low battery with `[[ \"${battery}\" \u003c 20 ]]`, which is a string (lexicographic) comparison. \"100\" sorts before \"20\", so a full battery gave WARN. \"9\" sorts after \"20\", so a nearly flat battery gave OK. Fix: use an arithmetic comparison, `(( 10#${battery} \u003c 20 ))`; the 10# prefix also keeps a zero-padded value from being read as octal. Added tests in test/run.sh for a 100% battery (no warning) and a 9% battery (warns). All 22 tests pass. Against data/status.txt, Skellan Rock is now OK and Dunvarrow Light is WARN (battery 9%)."
    }
  ]
}

